      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "There are several key factors contributing to the worsening wealth inequality in the U.S. over the past four decades:\n\n1. Technological advancements: Automation and digitalization have significantly boosted productivity in certain sectors but reduced demand for low-skill labor, contributing to wage stagnation.\n2. Declining labor unions: Fewer workers are represented by unions, reducing their bargaining power for better wages and benefits.\n3. Tax policies favoring the wealthy: Revised tax laws have often resulted in lower taxes for the richest Americans, allowing them to accumulate more wealth.\n4. Education gap: Growing disparities in access to quality education have hindered social mobility, with those in wealthy families enjoying better opportunities.\n5. Healthcare costs: Rising healthcare expenses, particularly due to medical innovations and fewer regulation changes, have disproportionately affected lower-income families, promoting wealth concentration at the top.\n\nThese factors, among others, have contributed to the growing wealth gap in the United States.",
